================================================================================
  napp-it CS -- status.pl: system status snapshots for AI evaluation
  File: data/howto.ai/status.info
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW
-----------
status.pl collects compact, read-only status snapshots of a napp-it node and
prints them as plain text -- designed to be handed to an AI for evaluation
(e.g. as AI Helpdesk context). It never modifies anything; every command is
status/health output only. No CPAN dependencies (File::Glob is core).

  Script:   /opt/csweb-gui/data/cs_server/tools/scripts/status.pl
  Short:    "status xxx"  -- a server.pl socket short command (cs_26.08.27),
            NOT a separate wrapper script. See section 8 for the exact
            protocol/implementation.

2. QUICK REFERENCE
------------------
    status pool      ZFS pool health + capacity + I/O + all pool properties
    status disk      smartmontools health of every detected disk
    status cs        all *.log files in /opt/csweb-gui/tmp (newest first)
    status jobs      all job files in /opt/csweb-gui/_cfg/jobs (newest first)
    status os        OS-specific status snapshot (OS auto-detected)
    status member    Clustergroup member roster: name/ip + TCP reachability
                     on port 45000 + 5-char auth-key fingerprint

3. COMMANDS IN DETAIL
---------------------

3.1 status pool -- storage pool status
  ZFS nodes (illumos/Solaris/FreeBSD/Linux):
    zpool status -v   pool state (ONLINE/DEGRADED/FAULTED), per-vdev READ/
                      WRITE/CKSUM error counters, last known errors, scrub
                      status -- the key diagnostic
    zpool list -v     size / alloc / free / CAP / FRAG / DEDUP / HEALTH,
                      plus the vdev layout below the pool
    zpool iostat -v   operations and bandwidth per pool and per vdev
    zpool get all     every pool property (ashift, autoreplace, expandsize,
                      dedupratio, compression, ...)
  Windows:            BOTH the ZFS status (zpool ... -- only if a ZFS build
                      such as O3X is present) AND the Storage Spaces
                      status: Get-StoragePool (pools + Health/Operational
                      status + size/allocation), Get-VirtualDisk (virtual
                      disks + ResiliencySettingName = Mirror/Parity/Simple
                      + NumberOfColumns), the physical disks inside the
                      pools (HealthStatus/MediaType), and Get-StorageJob
                      (running repair/scrub jobs).

3.2 status disk -- disk health via smartmontools
  Detects disks via `smartctl --scan`, then `smartctl -a` per disk:
    model / serial / firmware; SMART overall-health (PASSED/FAILED);
    reallocated-sector / current-pending counts (ATA) or the NVMe Health
    log (critical warning, available spare, % used, temperature); power-on
    hours, power cycles, media & data-integrity errors; self-test log.
  Windows particularity: disks are addressed as /dev/pdN (short for
    \\.\PhysicalDriveN; N = the Get-PhysicalDisk DeviceId, falling back to
    the --scan count). Without an elevated shell smartctl reports
    "Open failed: ... Error=5" (access denied) for all non-system disks.
  Returns "smartctl not found" if smartmontools is missing.

3.3 status cs -- napp-it web-GUI logs
  All *.log files in /opt/csweb-gui/tmp, newest first (by mtime), no *.lock
  files. The combined tail of:
    webserver.log / webserver_startup.log  HTTP + SSL handshake errors
    http.log                               per-request status + latency
    error.log                              socket/server errors
    watchdog.log                           webserver kill/restart events
    monitor.log / status.log               monitor + status checks
    status_history.log                     history counters
    admin_debug.log                        admin requests
    cs-aihelp.log                          AI Helpdesk activity/errors
    get_async.log / command.log / update.log / remote.log / s3.log /
    ai_savekey_debug.log / activity.log
  Each file is capped at the last 12000 chars.

3.4 status jobs -- job state and definitions
  All files in /opt/csweb-gui/_cfg/jobs, newest first, no *.lock files
  (jobs.log.lock, <jobid>.last.lock, ...). Filename + content of every
  <jobid>.final/.last/.log/.par/.progress/.receiver plus jobs.log, i.e.:
    .par        job definition/parameters
    .last       last run result
    .log        run log
    .final      request/final marker
    .progress   replication progress
    .receiver   replication receiver info
  Each file is capped at the last 12000 chars.

3.5 status os -- OS status snapshot
  Prefixes the output with "OS: <detected>", then returns the command set
  for the detected OS (see section 5). Read-only status, nothing changed.
  For INTERPRETING a "status os" snapshot (what is normal, red flags, how
  to structure the analysis) see data/howto.ai/status-os-guide.info.
  Every command set ends with a PROCESS SNAPSHOT (cs_26.08.27): the top
  processes by memory (ps --sort=-rss / ps -r / prstat / Get-Process) and
  the napp-it daemons webserver.pl / server.pl / monitor.pl / watchdog.pl
  (on Windows: the running perl.exe daemons) with PID + RSS.
  Windows additionally reports the System event-log errors (Level 1/2,
  last hour), the Kernel-Power events (41/1074/6008 -- reboot/crash
  cause) and the Application event-log errors (Level 1/2, last hour). All
  Get-WinEvent calls use -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue so an empty log is
  silently empty instead of raising "No events were found".

3.6 status member -- Clustergroup member roster (cs_26.08.27)
  Lists every configured Clustergroup member from
  /opt/csweb-gui/_cfg/group/*.txt filenames (localhost first, then sorted,
  *.lock files skipped) -- same source and order the "45_Clustergroup" GUI
  overview itself uses. For each member: name, ip, a plain TCP
  reachability probe on port 45000 (server.pl's own listen port; 2s
  timeout; "open"/"closed"/"error"), and a 5-character fingerprint of the
  member's shared auth secret (_cfg/group/<member>.txt's content) -- NEVER
  the full secret, matching exactly what the GUI overview itself already
  exposes (see 45_Clustergroup/action.pl's own "$t=substr($t,0,5)").
  This is a plain TCP connect check, NOT the encrypted "on"/&socket()
  status query the GUI overview uses for its os/cs_ver/zfs_ver/sync
  columns -- that needs socketlib.pl's crypto stack, deliberately not
  pulled into this dependency-free script. "closed"/"error" only tells you
  the member isn't reachable on that port; it does not distinguish offline
  vs. firewalled vs. wrong auth.

4. OS AUTO-DETECTION
--------------------
Windows is detected via Perl's $^O (MSWin32); all other systems via
`uname -s`:
  Linux    -> "proxmox" if /etc/pve exists or pveversion is present,
              otherwise "linux"
  FreeBSD  -> "freebsd"
  SunOS    -> "sunos"   (illumos / Solaris / SmartOS)
  Darwin   -> "darwin"  (macOS)
  anything else -> "unknown" (falls back to uname -a; uptime; df -h)

5. OS-SPECIFIC COMMAND SETS (status os)
---------------------------------------
FreeBSD:
  uname -a; uptime; sysctl hw.ncpu hw.physmem; top -b -d 1 | head -15;
  vmstat 1 2; swapinfo -h; df -h; zpool status -v; zpool iostat -v;
  iostat -x 1 2; netstat -i; dmesg | grep -iE 'error|fail' | tail -30;
  tail -40 /var/log/messages; for s in $(service -e); do service $s
  onestatus >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo "FAILED: $s"; done;
  grep -iE 'failed|authentication' /var/log/auth.log | tail -10;
  sysctl -a | grep -i temperature

Linux (generic):
  uptime; free -h; df -h; systemctl --failed 2>/dev/null;
  journalctl -p err -b --no-pager 2>/dev/null | tail -20;
  dmesg -T 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'error|fail' | tail -30;
  iostat -x 2>/dev/null || iostat 2>/dev/null

Proxmox VE (generic Linux status + Proxmox specifics):
  uptime; free -h; df -h; systemctl --failed 2>/dev/null;
  journalctl -p err -b --no-pager 2>/dev/null | tail -20;
  dmesg -T 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'error|fail' | tail -30;
  iostat -x 2>/dev/null || iostat 2>/dev/null;
  pveversion; pvecm status; qm list; pct list; pvesm status;
  journalctl -u pve-cluster -u corosync -u pveproxy -p err -b --no-pager
  | tail -20; ha-manager status

illumos / Solaris (fmd fault log included):
  uname -a; uptime; prtconf | grep -i memory; prstat -a 1 2 | head -15;
  vmstat 1 2; swap -l; df -h; iostat -xn 1 2; netstat -i;
  dmesg | grep -iE 'error|fail' | tail -30; tail -40 /var/adm/messages;
  fmadm faulty; fmdump -v | tail -20; svcs -xv | head -30;
  grep -iE 'failed|authentication' /var/adm/messages | tail -10

macOS:
  sw_vers; uname -a; uptime; sysctl -n machdep.cpu.brand_string hw.ncpu
  hw.memsize; vm_stat; top -l 1 -n 15 | head -15; df -h; iostat -d 1 2;
  netstat -i; log show --last 30m --style compact --level error | tail -40;
  dmesg | tail -30; launchctl list | grep -v com.apple; last | head -20

Windows (PowerShell):
  OS caption/version/build + uptime + RAM (total/free); CPU model + load;
  failed automatic services; top-15 processes by memory (RSS) plus the
  perl.exe napp-it daemons; System event-log errors of the last hour;
  Kernel-Power events (reboot/crash cause); Application event-log errors
  of the last hour; network adapter errors/discards. All read-only.

6. PARTICULARITIES
------------------
- status pool on Windows returns the ZFS status (zpool, if a ZFS build
  such as O3X is present) AND the Storage Spaces status (concatenated).
- status os on Proxmox returns the generic Linux status AND the Proxmox
  specifics (both blocks are concatenated).
- status disk on Windows uses /dev/pdN (PhysicalDriveN) -- see 3.2.
- illumos/Solaris os includes the fmd fault log (fmadm faulty + fmdump -v),
  macOS os the unified log (log show), Windows os the System event log.
- status os on every OS ends with a process snapshot (top memory + the
  napp-it daemons); Windows additionally reports the Kernel-Power and
  Application event logs. All Get-WinEvent calls use
  -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue (empty logs are silently empty, not an
  error).
- Output caps: "cs" and "jobs" cap each file at 12000 chars; pool/disk/os
  return the raw tool output.

7. NOTES / REQUIREMENTS
-----------------------
- Some parts need root/admin to produce full output (journalctl, fmdump,
  log show, Get-WinEvent on the Security log, smartctl on Windows
  PhysicalDrive devices). status.pl simply runs with whatever privileges
  the invoking user has.
- status disk locates smartctl via `which` (Unix) or PowerShell
  Get-Command (Windows); without an elevated Windows shell smartctl
  reports "Open failed: ... Error=5" (access denied) for non-system disks.
- On the illumos/FreeBSD server, make the script executable once:
      chmod +x /opt/csweb-gui/data/cs_server/tools/scripts/status.pl

8. SERVER.PL SHORT COMMAND -- "status xxx" (cs_26.08.27)
----------------------------------------------------------
Gea: "neuer kurzbefehl in server.pl: 'status xxx' mit xxx=pool,disk,os,cs,
jobs. server.pl ruft dann mit eval status.pl auf mit parameter und gibt
ergebnis zurueck." Same day, follow-up: "bitte noch status member
hinzufuegen (in status.pl integriert)" -- "member" added as a 6th class,
see 3.6. Follow-up "kernel + application bei status os (windows) mit
aufnehmen": Windows os now also reports the Kernel-Power and the
Application event logs (all Get-WinEvent calls with
-ErrorAction SilentlyContinue), and every os command set ends with a
process snapshot (top memory + napp-it daemons) -- see 3.5.
  Follow-up "Sind da systemlogs dabei, alle os eg dmesg probleme linux?":
  Linux/Proxmox previously only had journalctl -- dmesg -T (kernel ring
  buffer: OOM, I/O errors, hardware) is now included there too, matching
  FreeBSD/Solaris/macOS.

Implemented directly in server.pl's socket command dispatcher (the same
place as the existing "hi"/"on"/"mver" short commands), NOT as a separate
wrapper executable:

  Location:  data/cs_server/server.pl, in the "short cmds always processed
             directly after auth verification" block, right after the
             "mver" handler.
  Match:     $q =~ /^status\s+(pool|disk|os|cs|jobs|member)$/   -- a single
             whitelisted word after "status "; anything else (typo, extra
             args, unknown class) falls through, unmatched, to the normal
             command path instead of running status.pl.
  Call:      run [$^X, "$spath/tools/scripts/status.pl", $arg], \undef,
             \$out, \$err, timeout( STATUS_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECS );  -- IPC::Run
             with a hard 55s timeout (see the cs_26.08.27 UPDATE note
             below; originally a bare "eval { backticks }" with no
             timeout, changed after Gea asked "gibt es einen timeout fuer
             status.pl?"). $^X is the running Perl interpreter's own
             path, so this works whether perl is Strawberry (Windows) or
             the system perl (illumos/FreeBSD/Linux) without depending
             on PATH.
  Returns:   status.pl's plain-text stdout+stderr (2>&1) verbatim as the
             socket response, exactly like every other server.pl short
             command.

GOTCHA (cs_26.08.27, live-confirmed, cost real debugging time): matching
"status xxx" in server.pl is NOT enough on its own -- the CALLER-side
socketlib.pl (data/menues/_lib/windows/socketlib.pl, &socket()) decides
BEFORE sending whether a command goes as plain text or gets base64-wrapped
("b64cmd:..."), using its OWN separate whitelist regex (in 3 places in
that file):
  /^(?:hostname|hn|sc|rh|on|hi|os|dn|ip|fn|alive|stat|status|mem|mver|
     monitorupdate)\b/
This originally had "stat", NOT "status" -- and "stat\b" does NOT match
"status pool" (no word boundary between "stat" and the following "us").
Result: "status pool" got base64-encoded and arrived at server.pl as
"b64cmd:...", ONE STEP BEFORE server.pl's own "b64cmd:" decode (which
runs AFTER the "status xxx" match point) -- so the short-command match
never fired, and the (still-encoded, unrecognized) command fell all the
way through to the generic PowerShell exec fallback, which tried to run
"status" as a literal cmdlet name ("The term 'status' is not
recognized..."). Fixed by adding "status" to socketlib.pl's whitelist
regex in all 3 places. LESSON: any NEW server.pl short-command prefix
needs a matching entry in socketlib.pl's is_status whitelist too, or it
will silently get base64-wrapped and never reach its handler.

Trust model: "status xxx" is a FIXED, hardcoded command set -- the
argument is whitelisted to exactly the 6 known classes before status.pl is
even invoked, so nothing caller-supplied ever reaches a shell. It is
therefore in the same trust class as "hi"/"on"/"sc"/"mver" (always
answered after the socket's own auth+IP check, no further gate) -- it is
NOT part of, and not gated by, the AI-exec pipeline
(ai_exec_validate()/ai_exec_allowed()/exec_deny in aihelplib.pl) that
guards arbitrary AI-composed commands reaching server.pl's separate
"run cmd -> file" dispatcher further down. Unlike "hi"/"on" (excluded from
command.log to cut noise from per-refresh pings), "status" calls ARE
logged to command.log -- they are on-demand diagnostic calls, not a
polling heartbeat.

"status member" specifically: it reads _cfg/group/*.txt, the same files
that hold each Clustergroup member's shared auth secret (a single opaque
string, no other structure) -- see 3.6. Only a 5-char fingerprint is ever
returned, matching the existing Clustergroup GUI overview's own exposure
level exactly (grouplib's "45_Clustergroup/action.pl" already does the
identical substr(...,0,5) truncation for its "auth" column) -- this
command adds no new secret exposure beyond what any admin/operator
session could already see in that GUI.

Client-side call (from Perl, e.g. an action.pl), same &socket() used for
every other short command:
    my $out = &socket("status pool", $member_ip, 60);

UPDATE (cs_26.08.27, same day): the AI Helpdesk now DOES call this
automatically -- cs-aihelp.pl's action=status endpoint calls exactly the
&socket() line above (60s timeout) for one of the 6 classes, then wraps
the result into a prompt ("analyse <class> on <member>: ..." plus an
explicit "answer directly, do not propose further commands" instruction)
and forwards it to the AI via the normal /ask flow. Widget and full page
both show 6 small buttons (Pool/Disk/OS/CS/Jobs/Member,
ai_status_buttons_html() in aihelplib.pl) wired to this endpoint via JS
_aiStatusFetch(). See ai-helpdesk.info for the full UI/endpoint writeup.

UPDATE (cs_26.08.27, Gea: "gibt es einen timeout fuer status.pl?" / "wie
macht server.pl normalerweise timeout" / "ja"): the paragraph above (bare
backtick, no timeout) is OUTDATED -- status.pl's own subcommands have no
internal timeout of their own (only "status member"'s TCP reachability
probe uses one, 2s/member, see section 3.6 above), and a hang there used
to be able to block server.pl's single request thread forever. Switched
to the SAME IPC::Run pattern server.pl already uses for its general
Windows exec pipeline (the "ipc run" branches further down in server.pl,
loaded from the vendored data/cs_server/CGI/IPC/Run.pm via the @INC push
in the file's own BEGIN block -- no CPAN install needed, and confirmed
NOT present in the plain system Perl):

  run [$^X, $script, $arg], \undef, \$out, \$err, timeout( STATUS_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECS );

STATUS_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECS => 55 (new entry in server.pl's existing
"use constant {...}" block, next to SOCKET_TIMEOUT) -- a few seconds
under cs-aihelp.pl's own 60s socket timeout for action=status
(&socket("status $class", $ip, 60)), so a hang is always reported by
server.pl itself (a clear "status $arg timeout/error: ..." message) and
never left running as an orphaned process racing the caller's own
timeout. stderr is still merged into the returned text, matching the old
"2>&1" behaviour. Verified with a standalone script (same @INC push +
IPC::Run call against "status.pl os"): 5993 bytes returned, no error.
